{"abstract":"Objectives: To evaluate the roles of oxidative balance score (OBS) in staging and mortality risk of cardiovascular-kidney-metabolic syndrome (CKM). Methods: Data of this study were from the National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey 1999–2018. We performed cross-sectional analyses using multinomial logistic regression to investigate the relationship between OBS and CKM staging. Cox proportional hazards models were used to assess the impact of OBS on mortality outcomes in CKM patients. Additionally, mediation analyses were performed to explore whether OBS mediated the relationships between specific predictors (Life's Simple 7 score [LS7], systemic immune-inflammation index [SII], frailty score) and mortality outcomes. Then, machine learning models were developed to classify CKM stages 3/4 and predict all-cause mortality, with SHapley Additive exPlanations values used to interpret the contribution of OBS components. Results: 21,609 participants were included (20,319 CKM, median [IQR] age: 52.0 [38.0–65.0] years, 54.3% male, median [IQR] follow-up: 9.4 [5.3–14.1] years). Lower OBS quartiles were associated with advanced CKM staging. Moreover, lower OBS quartiles were related to increased mortality risk, compared to Q4 of OBS (all-cause mortality: Q1: HR 1.31, 95% CI 1.18–1.46, Q2: HR 1.27, 95% CI 1.14–1.42, Q3: HR 1.18, 95% CI 1.06–1.32; cardiovascular mortality: Q1: HR 1.44, 95% CI 1.16–1.79, Q2: HR 1.39, 95% CI 1.11–1.74, Q3: HR 1.26, 95% CI 1.01–1.57; non-cardiovascular mortality, Q1: HR 1.27, 95% CI 1.12–1.44, Q2: HR 1.23, 95% CI 1.08–1.40, Q3: HR 1.16, 95% CI 1.02–1.31), with optimal risk stratification threshold for OBS was 22. Additionally, OBS mediated (ranging 4.25%–32.85 %) effects of SII, LS7, frailty scores on mortality outcomes. Moreover, light gradient boosting machine achieved the highest performance for predicting advanced CKM staging (area under curve: 0.905) and all-cause mortality (area under curve: 0.875). Cotinine increased risk, while magnesium, vitamin B6, physical activity were protective. Conclusions: This study highlights OBS as a risk stratification tool for CKM, emphasizing oxidative stress's role in CKM staging and mortality risk management.","author_count":13,"contributors":["Chen, Yang","Wu, Shuang","Liu, Hongyu","Zhong, Ziyi","Bucci, Tommaso","Wang, Yimeng","Zhao, Manlin","Liu, Yang","Yang, Zhengkun","Gue, Ying","McDowell, Garry","Huang, Bi","Lip, Gregory Y H"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-805483"],"filters":{"added":"2025-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Asia, East","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Chen, Yang","Huang, Bi","Lip, Gregory Y H"],"country":["China","Italy","Poland","United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Beijing Anzhen Hospital","Capital Medical University","Chinese Academy of Medical Sciences","Chongqing Medical University","Fuwai Hospital","Liverpool Heart and Chest Hospital NHS Foundation Trust","Liverpool John Moores University","Medical University of Białystok","Ministry of Education, China","Nanchang University","Peking Union Medical College","The First Affiliated Hospital of Chongqing Medical University","Tianjin Medical University","University of Liverpool","University of Rome La Sapienza"],"keyword":["Mortality","Oxidative stress","Risk stratification"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"role of oxidative balance score in staging and mortality risk of cardiovascular kidney metabolic syndrome insights from traditional and machine learning approaches","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.redox.2025.103588","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-805483